WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.520
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers. Episode 112. Today is June 25th, 2026. Some fascinating developments

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.920
to cover today. Astronomers have captured the first fingerprints of a black hole's event horizon.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.940
The boundary where nothing escapes. Researchers identified subtle signatures in light that trace

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:24.660
the boundary where escape velocity equals the speed of light. The detection relied on high-resolution

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:30.460
observations of nearby, accreting material. The pattern matches predictions from general

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.560
relativity but had never been isolated before. The work used data from multiple ground-based

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.840
telescopes. Follow-up campaigns will search for the same signatures in other systems.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.000
The first detection of event horizon fingerprints come from careful analysis of light emitted by gas

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.820
spiraling near the boundary. Telescopes recorded tiny repeating brightness changes that match

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.520
computer models of photons scirting the point of no return. Because the signal is so faint,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.020
the team combined data from several observatories to pull it above the noise. The result gives

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:11.180
astronomers a new way to test whether real black holes behave exactly as predicted. What other

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.960
subtle signatures might still be hiding in existing observations of accreting systems.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.560
If you could shrink Earth's mass into a sphere just 1.8 centimeters across,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:27.940
its surface gravity would equal the speed of light and nothing could climb back out. Photons emitted

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:33.340
from that surface would lose all their energy trying to escape, stretching into infinite red shift.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:39.780
The actual event horizon of a stellar mass black hole sits only a few kilometers from its center.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.680
Yet the same rule applies. We can watch gas orbit just outside this boundary and see its light

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:52.860
dim and stretch. But we still cannot explain exactly how information about what fell in is preserved

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:59.800
or lost. On a different note, an analysis shows the cancelled exploration upper stage and gateway

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:06.000
elements were years behind schedule. Remember, the show covered Artemis on June 24th.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.940
Today's development clarifies why the agency shifted resources away from those elements.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:17.520
The Inspector General report details how NASA had already spent heavily on a simple Orion stage

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.960
adapter before termination. The move frees budget for other lunar landing hardware still in development.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.000
Watch for the next Inspector General update on remaining Artemis program milestones.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:35.200
Next up, the Euclid mission released its widest and sharpest view yet of the Galactic Center

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.180
in visible wavelengths. The image covers a crowded field packed with stars and dust that ground

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.360
telescope struggled to resolve. Euclid's wide field camera captured the data during routine survey

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.560
operations. Scientists will use the mosaic to map stellar populations and dust lanes across the

00:02:53.560 --> 00:03:00.320
inner galaxy. Future data releases will add near infrared layers to the same region. A new analysis

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.240
argues Canada can expand its role through targeted investments in robotics and earth observation.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.940
The report highlights existing strengths in satellite technology and astronaut contributions.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:18.240
It recommends closer coordination between government, universities, and industry. No specific

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.320
funding figures are attached to the proposals. Next steps depend on upcoming federal budget decisions.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.720
China's new rocket is scheduled to lift off in July from Wenchang.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.640
Engineers plan to attempt a controlled splashdown of the first stage at sea.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.680
The mission will test both the core vehicle and recovery hardware.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.320
Success would mark a step toward reusable heavy lift capability.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.500
Airspace notices have already been issued for the launch window.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.200
The orbiter that flew 25 missions now stands vertically at the California Science Center.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.160
Workers completed the final stacking and unveiling this week.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.560
The display shows endeavour made it with its external tank and solid rocket boosters.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.940
Visitors can view the vehicle from multiple levels inside the Samuel Ocean Pavilion.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.080
The installation marks the end of the shuttle's post-flight journey.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.240
The structure in Western Australia formed roughly 3 billion years ago.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:23.300
Earlier dating left its age uncertain, but new analysis of shock minerals and impact melt settled

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.040
the timeline. The creator's preservation offers a rare window into early Earth bombardment.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.140
Researchers compared it with younger, better studied sites to confirm the ancient date.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.480
Additional field work will search for similar old structures elsewhere.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.660
The planets are Jupiter-sized, yet have densities comparable to cotton candy.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:50.020
Data from the Transiting Exoplanet Survey satellite revealed their extremely low masses relative

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.880
to size. Follow-up observations refine the radius and mass measurements.

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.260
The world's orbit stars braid enough for additional atmospheric study.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.020
Researchers will next examine whether the low densities result from intense stellar heating.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.700
The satellite's cloud profiling radar is being assimilated into the European Center for

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:17.440
Medium-Range Weather Forecast system. This marks the first time space-based cloud radar observations

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:23.560
have been used operationally in a global model. The measurements help refine predictions of cloud

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:30.240
cover and precipitation. Data flow began after months of validation against ground-based instruments.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.840
Forecasters expect incremental gains in medium-range accuracy.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:41.120
The two-tone moon reaches 1.9 arc minutes due north of Saturn on June 25th.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.380
Iepidus is currently midway between its eastern and western elongations.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.780
Observers with small telescopes can watch the slow westward drift over coming weeks.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.020
The moon's unusual brightness contrast makes it easy to track against the planet's glare.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.860
The geometry repeats every 79 days. A team from the United States and Chile

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.880
found chemical traces left by the engulfed world.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.260
The star already hosts a brown dwarf companion that may have perturbed the planet's orbit.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:18.380
Spectra reveal unusual lithium and refractory element ratios consistent with planetary material.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.720
The event likely occurred within the past few million years.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:27.560
Additional radial velocity measurements will check for any remaining planets.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.960
A Korean team identified a pair locked in an orbit to rapid for current theory.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.220
One star appears to be stripping material from its companion at an unexpected rate.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.540
The system was caught during a slow, violent mass transfer phase.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.960
Spectra show unusual abundance patterns that standard models cannot reproduce.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.520
Continued monitoring will test whether the pair will merge or stabilize.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:58.280
Spring and summer 2026 brought a widespread bloom visible from orbit.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.760
The milky blue hue spread through the sea and connected waterways.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:07.560
Satellite imagery tracked the bloom's growth and movement over several weeks.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.600
Phytoplankton concentrations reached levels high enough to alter surface color dramatically.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:17.680
Similar events help scientists study nutrient cycling in enclosed basins.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:24.700
A new paper in science advances details unambiguous detection of organic molecules in two samples.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.040
The rocks were collected from the bright angel formation in September 2025.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.260
Instruments on the rover measured carbon abundance and molecular structure directly.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.800
The findings strengthen the case for past habitable conditions but do not prove biology.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.420
The samples remain cached for eventual return to earth.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.420
The program now accepts proposals from additional European launch companies.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:54.200
It aims to give small satellites and technology demonstrators regular rides.

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:57.920
Earlier rounds were limited to a smaller set of operators.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:02.720
Selected missions will receive funded flight opportunities on qualifying vehicles.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.460
The next call for proposals is expected later this year.

